     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical indication for 50 mg. At five times the maximum dose, the risk of serious harm is high. Amlodipine has a long half-life, so an overdose can cause hypotension and shock that last for days and are hard to reverse.\n\nHere are some ways I can help:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** For example, amlodipine 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily. Tell me which and I'll enter it.\n- **Add a note for rounds.** I can put a note on the order, such as \"titrate per team at evening rounds.\"\n- **Get a dose above the maximum approved.** If you have a specific clinical reason, the attending and pharmacy would need to review and approve it. I can't enter it on my own authority.\n\nIf your goal is faster blood pressure control, amlodipine reaches its full effect slowly, even at normal doses. A higher dose wouldn't speed that up. Other agents are better suited to rapid control, and the team or pharmacy can help choose one.\n\nWhich would you like me to do?"
      }
     ],
